  "diagnoses": {
    "DIAGNOSIS_T1": "Each histogram increment is a read-modify-write sequence, so concurrent workers can overwrite updates and lose counts. The weighted-sum update has the same race. Floating-point addition is not associative, so changing the grouping or order of additions can change low bits even when integer increments are atomic. The safe kernel uses private buffers and a fixed thread-index reduction; exact integer limbs make weighted totals independent of the OpenMP thread count.",
    "DIAGNOSIS_T2": "In the unpadded array, separate workers repeatedly write different int64 slots that share a 64-byte cache line. MESI coherence must transfer that line into each writer's exclusive state and invalidate other cores' copies, causing cache-line ping-pong despite the absence of a logical data race. Padding places each active slot on a distinct cache line while keeping the same recurrence and store frequency. The timing ratio is hardware- and runtime-dependent, so the recorded measurement is reported rather than assumed.",
    "DIAGNOSIS_T3": "A contiguous prange partition can assign neighboring rows to one worker, so a concentration of expensive rows near a partition boundary can leave other workers idle at the barrier. The balanced kernel uses a modular permutation with a stride coprime to the row count, distributing each worker's contiguous iteration block across the full workload. It retains the same per-row arithmetic and writes each result to its original row, so the numerical output is unchanged. Per-thread operation counters measure the achieved balance; this deterministic interleaving avoids requiring a contended shared atomic cursor."
  },
